## INTRODUCTION
Electrochemiluminescence (ECL) is a special chemiluminescence phenomenon of emitting light from the excited state of the luminophore produced via an electrochemical reaction.1,2 Benefiting from simple operation, low cost and high sensitivity, ECL technique has been widely applied in the field of clinical diagnosis, food safety and environmental monitoring.3-5 Generally, in comparison with the conventional ECL methods based on only one emission signal change, ECL ratiometric analysis has attracted increasing attention because of the advantage of precise measurement to restrain the signal interference by self-calibration of two emission signals, especially in a complex biological matrix.6,7 ECL report units with two emitting states are usually needed in the case of ratiometric ECL biosensors. Quantum dots (QDs) and luminol reagents, as cathodic and anodic emitters respectively, were extensively utilized in the ECL ratiometric assay.8-10 In these systems, hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ is always introduced as a coreactant to produce various reactive oxygen species (ROS), such as superoxide $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ and hydroxyl radical (OH•), which will facilitate the oxidation of luminophores and subsequently enhance the ECL signal.11-13 However, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is prone to decomposition at ambient temperature, which greatly affects the stability and reproducibility of bioanalysis.14-16 It is an effective way to seek green ECL emitters and durable coreactants as alternatives to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for the development of ratiometric ECL biosensor.
Porphyrin-based ECL system involving the dissolved $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ have been previously reported. Lu’s group described the ECL behaviors of porphyrins involving $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ participation, especially zinc porphyrin $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\mathrm{TPP})$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet^{-}}$ reacted directly with free radical anion of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})\mathrm{TPP}$ , resulting in cathodic ECL from singlet oxygen $(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ .17,18 However, these ECL behaviors of porphyrins involving dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were all realized in non-aqueous media, which largely limits their application in ECL biosensor. Shan and co-workers demonstrated the cathodic ECL mechanism of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ induced by the electroactive zinc porphyrin in aqueous media, which was the production of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the successive electrochemical and chemical reactions between ${\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{\bullet-}$ and intermediate anion of zinc $(Z\mathrm{n}^{\cdot+})$ porphyrin.19-21 Recently, constructed from porphyrins or their derivatives as ligands, porphyrinic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with highly ordered porosity and large specific surface area,22 have been extensively used for optoelectronic biosensing due to their enhanced chemical stability and photoelectric activity compared with porphyrin monomers. For example, Shen and Zhang et al.
reported two novel photoelectrochemical (PEC) immunosensors by using porphyrin-derived MOFs nanosphere (nPCN-224) and buckminsterfullerene coupled PCN-224 nanohybrid ( $\mathrm{\backslash}\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{\backslash}$ PCN-224), respectively.23,24 Shan’ group reported a “signal-on” ECL biosensor based on a $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOFs with zinc porphyrin inducing ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for protein kinase activity and inhibition assay.25 Feng’s group developed an ultrasensitive label-free ECL aptasensor for thrombin (TB) detection by grafting zinc proto-porphyrin IX $(\mathrm{ZnP})$ onto aminated zeolitic imidazole framework-8 as the luminophore.26 Additionally, porphyrinic MOFs are of readily biofunctionalized characters, especially oligonucleotide-functionalized MOF nanoparticles, which are favorable to bioanalysis.27-32 Thus, these advantages will contribute to the progress of green and ultrasensitive ECL biosensors. On the other hand, the dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in aqueous solution as an endogenous coreactant could be a more promising candidate due to its stability and nontoxicity compared with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , especially in luminol ECL system. Notably, the continuous adsorption and dissociation of dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules at the catalytic interface during the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) process would promote ECL reaction of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and luminol radical, resulting in a significant emission.33-37
Associated with the ECL behaviors of porphyrinic MOFs inducing ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , a competitive ECL catalytic reaction involving dissolved $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ may occur between porphyrinic MOFs and luminol. In this work, we propose dual-signal ratiometric ECL based on the porphyrinic MOFs-luminol competitive mechanism involving dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for the first time. As a proof-of-concept for ECL biosensor, DNA-functionalized zirconium-based porphyrinic MOF nanoparticles (NMOFs) were obtained via coordination binding between the unsaturated zirconium sites on NMOFs and the phosphonate groups from a consecutive stretch of guanines (poly(dG) spacer), which were effectively assembled on the modified electrode, as shown in Scheme 1, polynucleotide kinase (PNK), an important biomarker for nucleic acid metabolism,38-40 was detected by the competitive-mechanism-driven ratiometric ECL biosensor with a much higher sensitivity and broarder calibration curve range compared with that of single-signal-driven ECL methods. This work sheds light on the design of green and highly sensitive ECL biosensors.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and Apparatus. All the materials and apparatus used in this work were supplied in the Supporting Information.
Synthesis of NMOFs. The nanoscale PCN-224(Zn) (NMOFs) were synthesized according to the reported literature with modifications.41,42 Briefly, Zinc(II) meso-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphine (ZnTCPP, 10 mg, 0.013 mmol), ${\mathrm{ZrOCl}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(30\mathrm{\mg},\ 0.093\mathrm{\mmol})$ , and benzoic acid (280 mg, 2.3 mmol) were ultrasonically dissolved in DMF ( $\mathrm{10~mL})$ and heated to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven for $5\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, dark purple rod-shaped nanocrystals were harvested by centrifugation.
Preparation of P2-NMOFs. The as-synthesized NMOFs were redispersed into 0.5 mL of ultrapure water and incubated with1 $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) probe (P2, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ . The resulting solution was vigorously shaken overnight at room temperature. Free DNA was recovered from the P2-NMOFs suspension by washing and centrifugation. The obtained P2-NMOFs were dispersed in water/buffer for further work.
Fabrication of the PNK Assay. The fabrication procedure of the PNK assay is illustrated in Scheme 1. Before modification, the glass carbon electrode (GCE, $5\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter) was polished carefully with 0.3 and $0.05~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ of $\scriptstyle{a-\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}}$ powder, and washed sequentially with ethanol and double-distilled water in an ultrasonic bath. Then, the GCE was immersed in $10\ \mathrm{mM\HAuCl_{4}}$ solution for the electrodeposition of AuNPs film at $-0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ for $60~\mathrm{s}.$ The obtained AuNPs/GCE was incubated with $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.5~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ thiolated ssDNA probe (P1, dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mM}$ TE buffer containing 100 $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ tris(2-carboxyethyl) phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP), pH 7.4) at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . After rinsing with buffer solution, the working electrode was passivated with 1 mM 6-mercapto-1-hexanol (MCH) for $2\textrm{h}$ to block the unspecific sites and washed with the washing buffer thoroughly to obtain MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE modified electrode. Then, the modified GCE was incubated with $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of P2-NMOF conjugates in $10\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ containing 0.1 M NaCl for $2\mathrm{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After rinsing, the obtained P2-NMOF/MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE was incubated with phosphorylation reaction solution containing different concentrations of PNK and 1 mM ATP for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After rinsing, the $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.5\mathrm{~U~}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ lambda exonuclease ( $\lambda$ -Exo) was pipetted onto the above modified GCE and incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ . After rinsing thoroughly with double-distilled water, ${5\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $10~\mathrm{mM}$ surfactant tetraoctylammonium bromide (TOAB) was spread on the surface of the modified electrode and dried at ambient temperature, the electrodes were subjected to ECL measurements in the aqueous electrolyte solution containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ luminol. For inhibiting the activity of PNK, the different concentration PNK inhibitors were incubated with $10\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ PNK reaction buffer, followed by the similar experimental procedures for PNK assay described above. For real sample analysis, diluted HeLa (human cervical cancer cell) cell extracts (RayBiotech) were added to the PNK detection assay solution $(1\%)$ with the same procedure in pure reaction buffer.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of the NMOFs and DNA-NMOFs. NMOFs, Zr-based porphyrinic MOF nanoparticles, were synthesized according to a reported bottom-up approach with modifications.39,40 Then, on the basis of the intrinsic coordination binding zirconium to phosphate groups, especially “Zr-( $\mathrm{OPO}_{3}$ -poly(dG) DNA)” coordination effect,43-45 t he reporter ssDNA probe (P2)-conjugated NMOFs (P2-NMOFs) were prepared by employing $3^{\prime}$ -polyG-phosphate-terminal P2 through coordination of its phosphate groups with the unsaturated sites of $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ cluster on the surface of NMOFs (Figure 1A). X-ray diffraction (XRD) confirmed the well-defined crystal structure of the as-synthesized PCN-224 nanoparticles (Figure S1). TEM (Figure 1B) and SEM (Figure S2) images of the NMOFs show that they are uniform spherical with mean sizes of ${\sim}100~\mathrm{nm}$ . The obtained P2-NMOFs remained spherical shape, and the particle size was larger about $112\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 1C). Dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurements demonstrated that the hydrodynamic particle size of the NMOFs changed from 102 to $118~\mathrm{{nm}}$ after P2 conjugation (Figure 1D). This $16\ \mathrm{nm}$ increase in diameter is consistent with the addition of a ssDNA loading onto the NMOFs. Additionally, NMOFs showed a positively charged zeta potential $(12~\mathrm{mV})$ , and became highly negatively charged $(-21~\mathrm{\mV})$ after P2 conjugation (inset of Figure 1D), attributing to the negatively charged ssDNA.
Moreover, the UV-visible and PL emission spectra of NMOFs and P2-NMOFs dispersed in water was shown in Figure 1E. A typical Soret band at $422~\mathrm{nm}$ tailing with weak Q band absorption located between 540 and $650~\mathrm{nm}$ were observed in the UV-visible spectrum of NMOFs (Figure 1E, curve a, solid), derived from ZnTCPP ligands assembled on NMOFs. In comparison with NMOFs, the UV-visible spectrum of P2-NMOFs simultaneously displayed the characteristic absorption peaks of NMOFs and strong UV absorption near $250~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ (Figure 1E, curve b, solid), corresponding to the ssDNA loaded on the NMOFs surface. Meanwhile, the PL emission spectra of NMOFs and P2-NMOFs show the same characteristic peaks containning a strong peak at $614~\mathrm{nm}$ and a lesser at $742~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1E, curve a, b, dashed), indicated that the conjugation of P2 has no effect on the photochemical property of NMOFs. In addition, the stability of NMOFs was studied before and after DNA conjugation. The inset of Figure 1E shows the pictures of NMOFs and P2-NMOFs dispersed in water. P2-NMOFs displayed much better stability after overnight than NMOFs without any surface modification, suggested that DNA conjugation can significantly enhance the biostability of NMOFs in aqueous phase system and promote their application in biosenors.
ECL Investigations. Firstly, ECL properties of NMOFs in aqueous media were investigated. The ECL spectrum of surfactant TOAB activated NMOFs modified GCE (TOAB/NMOFs/GCE) exhibited the characteristic ECL emission at $633~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3), derived from the 1O2 dimol state involving collisions of two 1O2 molecules.46 Meanwhile, the ECL-time response of modified electrode during a continuous cathode potential scan displayed constant and stable signals, indicating the excellent stability of the cathodic ECL process owning to the highly regular porous structure of NMOFs for the enrichment of dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules (Figure S4A). However, the introduction of $\mathrm{NaN}_{3}$ , a quenching agent of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulted in the gradual decrease of
ECL signal (Figure S4B). These results are consistent with the previous reports,19,25 which confirm that the photoelectric porphyrinic MOFs reduce the dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into ROS and produce ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , resulting in cathodic ECL signal. Secondly, the anodic ECL of luminol can be dramatically promoted by uniting the electrochemical reduction of dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and electrochemical oxidation for the effectively trigger of anodic ECL emission.33-37 The reaction behavior involving ROS exists in both ECL systems. Thus, considering the combination of these two ECL reaction mechanisms, the electrochemical and ECL behaviors of NMOFs-luminol system were investigated in aqueous media. The typical cyclic voltammetry (CV) and ECL responses of TOAB/NMOFs/GCE modified electrode in the $10\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{pH}\ 7.4$ HEPES containing different concentrations of luminol were displayed in Figure 2A and 2B. Specifically, the modified electrode showed obvious reduction peak of dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ near $-0.5\mathrm{~V},$ and the intensity of peak current was independent of the concentration of luminol. With the increase of luminol concentration, the reduction current of modified electrode increased from $-0.5\mathrm{~V}_{;}$ , and the anodic current also increased, derived from luminol involving in the oxidation and reduction process. Simultaneously, a significant cathodic ECL peak at $-1.55\mathrm{~V~}$ of modified electrode was observed in the electrolyte without luminol (Figure 2B, curve a), attributed to the generation of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . With the increase of luminol concentration, the anodic ECL intensity gradually increased, conversely, the cathodic ECL response decreased (Figure 2B, curve b, c), highlighting the possibility of competitive catalytic reaction in this NMOFs-luminol ECL system. To verify this point, we further investigated the effect of luminol at different concentrations on the cathodic and anodic ECL responses. As shown in Figure 2C, the anodic ECL $\mathrm{(ECL_{a,})}$ intensity of luminol exhibited a gradual enhancement and the cathodic ECL $\mathrm{(ECL_{c})}$ signal from ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decreased with the increasing amounts of luminol from 0 to $5{\times}10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ . And, the logarithm of the ratio of anodic to cathodic ECL intensity $\mathrm{(ECL_{a}/E C L_{c})}$ ) linearly corresponded to the logarithm of luminol concentration from $1\times{10}^{-5}$ to ${5}{\times}{10}^{-5}$ M (inset of Figure 2C). These ratiometric signal responses indicate that a competitive ECL reaction occurs between NMOFs and luminol. Then, the influence of dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on the NMOFs-luminol ECL system was investigated. As exhibited in Figure 2D, the modified electrode showed relatively weak ECL response after 10 minutes of $\Nu_{2}$ injection into the solution (Figure 2D, curve b), showed that dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ participates in reaction and enhances the ECL signal of NMOFs-luminol system. Finally, the modified electrode upon continuous cyclic scans showed constant and stable ECL signals with relative standard deviation (RSD) of $2.3\%$ (Figure 2E), which demonstrated the competitive ECL reaction involving dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can achieve balance and excellent stability. Therefore, this competitive system can provide a platform for the construction of ECL sensors with high sensitivity and stability.
