Construction and Feasibility of the ECL Biosensor. T4 PNK is a primary member of $5^{\prime}$ -kinase family which can repair the $5^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl radical of nucleic acids by catalyzing the transfer of the γ-phosphate residue from ATP to the $5^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of oligonucleotides.47 The abnormalities in PNK activity will result in serious diseases, such as loom’s, Werner, and Rothmund-Thomson syndromes.39,48 Therefore, it is of great significance to monitor PNK activity for clinical diagnoses and fundamental biochemical research. The designed dual-signal ECL ratiometric strategy based on the NMOFs-luminol competitive system would be used for highly sensitive detection of PNK activity. The P2-NMOFs were assembled on the modified electrode and the procedure for the preparation of PNK biosensor was illustrated in Scheme 1.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was used to characterize the self-assembled process of the modified electrode. The semicircle diameter of Nyquist plot represents the charge transfer $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , related to the difficulty of electron transfer of a ferricyanide-redox probe between the elecrtode and the solution. As shown in Figure 3A, when AuNPs were electrodeposited onto the surface of GCE, the semicircle diameter decreased significantly and impedance diagram became a straight line (Figure 3A, curve b), attributed to the excellent electrical conductivity of AuNPs. After assembling the thiolated P1 and MCH, continuously, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ increased successively (Figure 3A, curve c and d), owing to the electronic repulsion between ${\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}{}^{4-/3-}$ and the negative charges on the DNA phosphoric acid backbone, and the alkanethiol of MCH blocked interfacial electron transfer. However, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value decreased when the P2-NMOFs was assembled on the MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE (Figure 3A, curve e). This decrease can be attributed to the electron transfer capacity of the modified electrode improved by NMOFs.22,43 After the reaction of the P2-NMOFs/MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE with $0.5\mathrm{~U~}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ -Exo, 1 mM ATP, and $0.1\mathrm{~U~}$ $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ PNK, an increase of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value was observed due to the dissociation of P2-NMOFs from the surface of modified electrode induced by the digestion of the phosphorylated double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) by $\lambda$ -Exo. Subsequently, the introduction of the surfactant TOAB caused a further increase on the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value (Figure 3A, curve g). Therefore, these results demonstrate the successful fabrication of the ECL biosensor.
Meanwhile, the ECL responses of three key modified electrodes were measured and shown in Figure 3B. Only a strong anodic ECL peak of TOAB/MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE was observed (Figure 3B, curve a), originating from luminescence of luminol. After the incubation of MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE with
P2-NMOFs, the TOAB/P2-NMOFs/MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE exhibited a strong cathodic ECL peak and a marked decrease of the anodic ECL peak (Figure 3B, curve b), because the introduction of NMOFs leads to the production of competitive ECL phenomenon. However, P2-NMOFs/MCH/P1/AuNPs/GCE after reaction with $0.5~\mathrm{U}$ $\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ -Exo, 1 mM ATP, and $0.1\mathrm{~U~mL^{-1}}$ PNK, modified by TOAB, the cathodic ECL peak decreased by $51.6\%$ , but the anodic ECL signal of luminol increased by $470.4\%$ (Figure 3B, curve c), indicating that luminol dominated the competitive ECL system due to the partial reduction of the amount of P2-NMOFs on the interface, suggesting the feasibility of this dual-potential ECL ratiometric biosensor for PNK assay.
Ratiometric Detection of PNK. After optimization of the assay conditions, such as the concentration of P1, the concentration of ATP, the phosphorylation reaction time, and $\lambda$ -Exo concentration (Figure S5), PNK activity was evaluated by the proposed dual-signal ECL ratiometric biosensor. With the increasing amounts of PNK (from 0 to $20.0\ \mathrm{U\mL}^{-1}$ ), the $\mathrm{ECL_{a}}$ intensity of luminol exhibited a gradual enhancement and the $\mathrm{ECL_{c}}$ signal from ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decreased simultaneously (Figure 4A), which indicated the good capability of the biosensor in response to the target. As shown in inset of Figure 4A, the ECL intensity was dependent on the logarithm of the PNK concentration with the respective calibration curves. Based on a signal-to-noise $(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N})$ of 3, the limits of detection (LOD) of these two ECL signals were estimated to be $1.8{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}(R^{2}$ $=0.9864)$ and $8.7{\times}10^{-5}\mathrm{~U~mL}^{-1}$ $R^{2}=0.9799)$ . As a result, the logarithm of the ratio of anodic to cathodic ECL intensity $\mathrm{(ECL_{a}/E C L_{c})}$ ) linearly corresponded to the logarithm of PNK concentration from 0.0002 to $10\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3B). The regression equation was $\mathrm{lg(ECL_{a}/E C L_{c})}=0.7513+0.3571\mathrm{lg}C_{\mathrm{PNK}}(R^{2}=0.989$ , and the LOD was estimated to be $6.5{\times}10^{-5}~\mathrm{~U~}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $\left(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}\ =\ 3\right)$ ). Meanwhile, instead of the competitive NMOFs-luminol ECL system, the analytical performance of PNK sensor was evaluated only by the cathodic ECL response generated from the catalytic reaction of NMOFs. The cathodic ECL decreased with increasing the concentration of PNK (Figure S6A), ascribing to the gradual reduction of the amount of NMOFs on the sensing interface. And the ECL intensity exhibits a linear correlation with the logarithm of PNK concentration from 0.001 to $10\ \mathrm{U\mL^{-1}}$ (Figure S6B). The LOD was estimated to be $3.2{\times}10^{-4}\ \mathrm{U\mL}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}=3)$ ). Obviously, it is inferior to the competitive NMOFs-luminol ECL system. The comparison results reveal that this dual-signal mode exhibits higher accuracy, sensitivity, and $R^{2}$ value, demonstrating great superiority of the ratiometric ECL biosensor. Moreover, the analytical performance of this proposed ECL ratiometric biosensor behaves better than most of the recently reported works (Table S1). The improved sensitivity of this ECL biosensor can be attributed to the competitive ECL mechanism involving dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ between NMOFs and luminol. Furthermore, it is worth noting that the proposed method only needs dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ instead of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as a coreactant compared with the reported competitive-mechanism-driven sensing strategies,8-11,13 which is of great importance for the development of green ECL biosensing system.
Specificity, Stability, Repeatability and Inhibition Assay. Some interfering proteins containing bovine serum albumin (BSA), protein kinase A (PKA), lysozyme, E. coli ligase, and the heat-inactivated T4 PNK were chosen to investigate the specificity of the ECL ratiometric biosensor. Only in the presence of PNK was the large $\mathrm{ECL_{a}/E C L_{c}}$ ratio observed, and the interfering proteins had no obvious effect on the ratios of the ECL intensity (Figure 5A), revealing the excellent specificity of the proposed biosensor. No significant change in ECL response was observed when the biosensor was tested in the pH 7.4 HEPES solution containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ luminol upon 10 cycles of continuous cyclic scans from $0\mathrm{V}$ to $-1.7\mathrm{V}$ to $0.7\mathrm{V}$ to $0\mathrm{v}$ (Figure S7), indicating the excellent ECL potential cycling stability for PNK assay. The reproducibility of biosensor was evaluated by measuring $1~\mathrm{U~mL}^{-1}$ of PNK 10 times under the same conditions and the variation coefficient was $5.23\%$ , which demonstrates an acceptable reproducibility. Meanwhile, ADP and $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}S O_{4}}$ as common inhibitors49,50 were investigated to evaluate the PNK inhibition activity (Figure 5B). The half-maximal inhibition concentration $\left(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\right)$ values for ADP and $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ were calculated to be 1.08 and $14.45~\mathrm{\mM}$ , respectively, which are comparable with the results obtained in the reported literatures,51-53 and confirm the feasibility of the constructed ECL ratiometric biosensor to screen PNK inhibitors.
Application in Real Sample Analysis. To evaluate the feasibility of the present biosensor’s application in real sample analysis, the ECL responses produced by different PNK activities in reaction buffer containing $1\%$ (v/v) HeLa cell extracts were similar to those acquired in clean reaction buffer (Figure S8), confirming that it is capable to quantitatively detect PNK sensitively in complex biological samples by using the simultaneously constructed calibration curve in a clean buffer system. This result demonstrates that the proposed ECL biosensor holds great promise in clinical diagnosis and biomedical research.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have demonstrated a novel dual-signal ECL ratiometric strategy for highly sensitive PNK assay based on a competitive reaction between porphyrinic MOF nanoparticles and luminol by assembling DNA-functionalized NMOFs on the sensing interface. The competitive ECL mechanism of NMOFs-luminol system involving oxygen radicals was systematically studied. Moreover, the detection sensitivity of the ratiometric PNK biosensor based on the NMOFs-luminol competitive ECL system was also inherently boosted compared with that of single-signal-driven ECL assay, demonstrating great superiority of the proposed method. Additionally, the proposed ECL biosensor with high selectivity, good repeatability, and excellent stability can further used to screen the potential PNK inhibitors and accurately quantify the PNK activity in complex biological samples, holding great potential for disease diagnosis and drug discovery. Furthermore, this typical MOFs-based competitive ECL mechanism involving dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ would provide new prospects for further advance of green and highly sensitive ECL detection systems.
